Ladybird’s text-shadow layers shadow the text’s decoration lines as
well as its glyphs, and the lines of an outer decorating box are painted
below those of the boxes nested in it. Blink does the same: Its
TextDecorationPainter paints the lines in TextShadowPaintPhase::kShadow
too, and so does Gecko’s nsTextFrame::PaintOneShadow(), which draws
the decorations along with the run in the shadow’s color. Both paint
the glyphs’ and the lines’ shadows one after the other, so where they
overlap, a translucent shadow comes out darker, as it does in Ladybird.
WebKit’s TextDecorationPainter paints the underline and overline once
for each of its m_shadow layers, but not the line-through. Chrome 158
and Firefox 151 shadow the underline, overline, and line-through,
blurred or not, and the lines a decorating ancestor propagates to a
shadowed span; WebKit 26.5 shadows the underline and overline only. None
of them shadows the line-through that a ::selection adds with the
originating text’s own shadow.
